    "answer": {
      "short": "$\\frac{1}{2}$.\n\nSwapping black and white pairs the two complementary events with equal probability.",
      "worked": "Swapping black and white in any sequence of three draws gives an equally likely sequence, since the colours are equally likely and the draws are made with replacement. This pairs every sequence with at most one black marble with one having at least two black marbles. These two events are complementary and equally likely, so each has probability $\\frac{1}{2}$.",
